.md
# Embed cell views
If you paste a nice-looking Markdown table, it will clash with some Markdown syntax reserved to separate slides. However, you can use `CellView` to embed a markdown cell into a slide instead:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
# Embed cell views
If you paste a nice-looking Markdown table, it will clash with some Markdown syntax reserved to separate slides. However, you can use `CellView` to embed a markdown cell into a slide instead:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Fade: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
MDTable = CellView[Cell["
| Material                            | V ${}^\\circ/(\\textrm{T}~\\textrm{cm})$ | $\\lambda$ (nm) | Ref.                                 |
|-------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------|
| Bilayer semiconductor MoSe2         | $10^7$                                 | 800             | Benjamin Carey et al. Nature Comm. 2024 15 |
| Nano particles PS-CoNP              | $10^3$                                 | 1310            | Chemistry of Materials 2022 34           |
| Pr$^{3+}$ doped glass                    | $35$                                     | 1310            |                                           |
| Topological insulator Bi$_2$Se$_3$  | $1000$                        | 8265            | L. Ohnoutek Scientific Reports 6 2016    |
| FePS3 antiferromagnet              | $629$                                    | 68879           | Kartik P. et al. arxiv 2025              |
| Helimagnet CuFe$_{1-x}$Ga$_x$O$_2$ | $450$                                    | $10^6$         | S. Kibayash et al. NATURE COMM. 5:4583   |
", "Output", "markdown"]];

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.slide

<MDTable/>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: slide
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
<dummy >FrontEndExecutable[F7fd03acd-d19f-45d0-b13c-d1593789fcc3]</dummy>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
The same counts for Mermaid diagrams:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
The same counts for Mermaid diagrams:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
MermaidDiagram = CellView[Cell["
  graph LR
      A[Text Header] --> B[Binary Header]
      B --> C1[Trace 1] --> T1[Samples 1]
      B --> C2[Trace 2] --> T2[Samples 2]
", "Output", "mermaid"],ImageSize -> 650];

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.slide

<MermaidDiagram/>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: slide
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
<dummy >FrontEndExecutable[F6e6dbedd-1332-4158-b725-a4082044eaf5]</dummy>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## Render Wolfram source code
Using `EditorView` you can display chunks of Wolfram Language code in your narratives. Here is a helper wrapper:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Render Wolfram source code
Using `EditorView` you can display chunks of Wolfram Language code in your narratives. Here is a helper wrapper:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.wlx
CodeInset[str_String, OptionsPattern[]] := With[{Ev = EditorView[str], size = OptionValue["FontSize"]},
  <div style="text-align: left; font-size:{size}px"><Ev/></div>
];

Options[CodeInset] = {"FontSize" -> 14};

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Then we can place any valid input expression, which will be directly rendered on a slide:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Then we can place any valid input expression, which will be directly rendered on a slide:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.slide
## Source code

<CodeInset FontSize={14}>
1-((*FB[*)((1)(*,*)/(*,*)(6))(*]FB*)) ((*SpB[*)Power[x(*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*))+((*FB[*)((1)(*,*)/(*,*)(120))(*]FB*)) ((*SpB[*)Power[x(*|*),(*|*)4](*]SpB*))-(*FB[*)(((*SpB[*)Power[x(*|*),(*|*)6](*]SpB*))(*,*)/(*,*)(5040))(*]FB*)+(*FB[*)(((*SpB[*)Power[x(*|*),(*|*)8](*]SpB*))(*,*)/(*,*)(362880))(*]FB*)-(*FB[*)(((*SpB[*)Power[x(*|*),(*|*)10](*]SpB*))(*,*)/(*,*)(39916800))(*]FB*)
</CodeInset>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: slide
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
<dummy >## Source code

<div style="text-align: left; font-size:14px">FrontEndExecutable[Fa1a7505f-0113-4259-be88-f1b56de8b828]</div></dummy>

